Our Java singleton occasionally creates two instances under load:
```java
if (instance == null) {
  synchronized (Singleton.class) {
    instance = new Singleton();
  }
}
return instance;
```
We thought synchronized fixed it.

Java cache warm-up sometimes throws weird errors and loses entries. We use a plain HashMap filled by 8 threads at startup:
```java
Map<String,Config> cache = new HashMap<>();
// 8 threads put() concurrently
```
Single-threaded it is perfect.

Our backoff doesn't back off. Retry delays measured in logs: 1s, 1s, 1s, 1s, 1s. Code:
```python
for attempt in range(5):
    delay = 1
    try:
        return call()
    except TransientError:
        time.sleep(delay)
        delay *= 2
```
The `delay *= 2` line is right there — why is it always 1?

Go service: worker count is fixed at 50, but `runtime.NumGoroutine()` in pprof climbs from 200 to 40k over a day. Workers do:
```go
func worker(jobs <-chan Job, results chan<- Result) {
  for j := range jobs { results <- process(j) }
}
```
The results consumer only runs during a nightly aggregation. CPU is idle, memory keeps growing.

We added a simple cache and now OOM every few days:
```js
const cache = new Map();
app.get('/search', (req,res)=>{
  const q = req.query.q;
  if (!cache.has(q)) cache.set(q, doSearch(q));
  res.json(cache.get(q));
});
```
Traffic is normal search traffic. Heap dumps show the Map dominating.

Java service on Tomcat: heap grows slowly, and worse, we occasionally see one tenant's user ID in another request's logs. We set context like:
```java
static final ThreadLocal<UserCtx> CTX = new ThreadLocal<>();
// filter: CTX.set(new UserCtx(userId));  // never removed
```
Threads come from the container pool. Two symptoms, one cause?
